 "facts": [
  {
   "claim": "Klimt began the portrait in 1903 and it is dated 1907; medium oil, gold and silver on canvas",
   "source": "https://neuegalerie.org/womaningold ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Adele_Bloch-Bauer_I"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Commissioned by Adele's husband Ferdinand Bloch-Bauer, a Jewish sugar magnate",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Adele_Bloch-Bauer_I ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinand_Bloch-Bauer"
  },
  {
   "claim": "After the Anschluss in 1938 Ferdinand fled Austria; the portrait was one of five Klimt paintings seized by the Nazis from the Bloch-Bauer home",
   "source": "https://neuegalerie.org/womaningold ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinand_Bloch-Bauer"
  },
  {
   "claim": "In 1941 it was transferred to the Austrian State Gallery (Österreichische Galerie Belvedere) in Vienna, which kept it until 2006",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Adele_Bloch-Bauer_I"
  },
  {
   "claim": "It was retitled 'Dame in Gold' / 'Woman in Gold' (Lady in Gold) to conceal the sitter's identity / remove reference to its Jewish subject",
   "source": "https://neuegalerie.org/womaningold ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Adele_Bloch-Bauer_I ; https://www.huffpost.com/entry/maria-altmann-woman-in-gold_n_7065102"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Maria Altmann (born 18 Feb 1916), Adele's niece, fled after the 1938 Anschluss and settled in the US (Los Angeles)",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_Altmann"
  },
  {
   "claim": "She sued Austria in US federal court in 2000 (aged 84); the case reached the US Supreme Court (Republic of Austria v. Altmann, 2004)",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_Altmann ; https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/541/677/"
  },
  {
   "claim": "In January 2006 an Austrian arbitration panel ruled the paintings must be returned; Austria returned them in 2006",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_Altmann ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Adele_Bloch-Bauer_I ; https://www.huffpost.com/entry/maria-altmann-woman-in-gold_n_7065102"
  },
  {
   "claim": "In June 2006 Ronald S. Lauder bought it for the Neue Galerie for US$135 million, then a record price for a painting",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Adele_Bloch-Bauer_I ; https://neuegalerie.org/womaningold"
  },
  {
   "claim": "It is on permanent view at the Neue Galerie, New York, titled Adele Bloch-Bauer I",
   "source": "https://neuegalerie.org/womaningold"
  },
  {
   "claim": "The Neue Galerie is at 1048 Fifth Avenue (at 86th Street), New York City",
   "source": "https://www.neuegalerie.org/ ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neue_Galerie_New_York"
  }
 ],
 "caption": "The Nazis stole this portrait, and erased her name.\n\nIn 1907 Gustav Klimt finished a portrait of Adele Bloch-Bauer in oil, silver and gold, paid for by her husband Ferdinand, a Jewish sugar magnate. In 1938 the Nazis took Austria. Ferdinand fled, and his Klimt paintings were seized.\n\nThe portrait ended up in Vienna's state gallery under a new title: \"Lady in Gold\" (Dame in Gold). No name, nothing to say the woman in it was Jewish.\n\nHer niece, Maria Altmann, had escaped to America. In 2000, at 84, she sued Austria; the case went all the way to the US Supreme Court. In 2006 an Austrian arbitration panel ruled for her, and the paintings came back to the family. That June, Ronald Lauder bought the portrait for $135 million, a record at the time, and it got its name back: Adele Bloch-Bauer I. It hangs in the Neue Galerie, on Fifth Avenue in New York.\n\n🖤 Should every looted painting go home?",
 "caption_short": "The Nazis stole this Klimt and erased her name. Her niece fought Austria for 6 years to get it back. 🖤",
 "youtube_title": "Who erased her name? | Klimt, Adele Bloch-Bauer I",
